            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 2
                  %mi d
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2d
            along one of the axes.
          %p
            Now the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bianchi+identity"} Bianchi identity
            for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/RR-field"} RR-field
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flux+density"} flux density
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mn 5
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_5
            sourced by any
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D3-branes"} D3-branes
            is
          #eq:BianchiIdentityForD3InBetweenNS5AndD5.maruku-equation
            %span.maruku-eq-number (8)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} d
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mn 5
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo =
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %msub
                    %mi H
                    %mn 3
                  %mo ∧
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mn 3
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\mathrm{d} \, F_{5}
                  \\;=\;
                  H_3 \wedge F_3
                  \\,,
          %p
            Imposing the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/boundary+condition"} boundary condition
            that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mn 5
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_5
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vanishing+at+infinity"} vanishes at infinity
            = succeed "-flux" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi F
                      %mn 5
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_5
            = succeed "-branes" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                    %mn 3
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D3
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/wedge+product"} wedge product
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi H
                    %mn 3
                  %mo ∧
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mn 3
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H_3 \wedge F_3
            has the form of a “multipole” roughly supported in between these branes.
          %p The following graphics, which shows a 2-dimensional shadow of the situation, qualitatively indicates that and why this is the case:
          %center
            %div{:style => "margin:-40px 10px 10px 0;"}
              %img{:src => "/nlab/files/FivebraneDipole-230620b.jpg", :width => "580"}/
              %img/
          %p Here
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                the strength of the circular lines indicates the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/absolute+value"} absolute value
                of the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flux+densities"} flux densities
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi H
                          %mn 3
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H_3
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi F
                        %mn 3
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_3
                (vanishing at infinity and spiked at the locus of the sourcing 5-brane),
            %li
              %p
                the little arrows indicate the
                %span.newWikiWord
                  orientation
                  %a{:href => "/nlab/new/orientation+of+a+vector"} ?
                of the two flux densities, projected to the plane that is being shown
            %li
              %p
                the little parallelograms indicate the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orientation"} orientation
                of the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/wedge+product"} wedge product
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi H
                        %mn 3
                      %mo ∧
                      %msub
                        %mi F
                        %mn 3
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H_3 \wedge F_3
                in the projection to the plane being shown.
          %p
            (Hence what the graphics is really showing, approximately, is the wedge product of a pair of translates of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi dvol
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi S
                        %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} dvol_{S^1}
            pulled back to around either puncture of the 2-punctured plane.)
          %p
            The point is to observe that — besides the abolute value of the wedge product
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mn 3
                  %mo ∧
                  %msub
                    %mi H
                    %mn 3
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_3 \wedge H_3
            evidently being concentrated near the branes, hence particularly between them when they are close — the sign of the orientation of the wedge product changes as the transverse coordinate passes through the axis between the branes.
          %p
            Hence as we solve the differential equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} d
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mn 5
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo =
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %msub
                    %mi H
                    %mn 3
                  %mo ∧
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mn 3
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{d} F_5 \,=\, H_3 \wedge F_3
            incrementally by running, say, from the top to the bottom in the above graphics, we see that the absolute value of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mn 5
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_5
            — indicated in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathcolor => "red"}
                    %mtext red shading
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \color{red}\text{red shading}
            above — fist incrementally picks up positive contributions as we approach the area between the branes, and then dimishies again as we leave that area. In total, the absolute value of the solution
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mn 5
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_5
            will be supported roughly in between the two 5-branes.
          %p
            Since
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mn 5
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_5
            is the flux sourced by
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D3-branes"} D3-branes
          %p However, from this analysis of the fluxes alone, it does not follow that the D3-branes would disappear as the NS5- and D5-brane “swap positions”, as Hanany-Witten would suggest. In fact there is no intrinsic such directedness in the problem in the first place with respect to which one could even speak about the branes “swapping positions”.
          %p
            Hence to see the full Hanany-Witten effect in this case, if it exists, one may have to look beyond the bare fluxes and include also the field of gravity. A suggestion for how this might work is given, for the analogous situation of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M2-M5+brane+bound+state"} M2-M5 brane
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/brane+intersection"} intersections
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#Hosomichi00"} Hosomichi (2000), §5
          %p
            %br/
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %div
            %p
